<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Evaluation: IPSS with the quality-of-life question, bladder scan for post-void residual, urinalysis and urine culture, blood count, creatinine and electrolytes, digital rectal examination, and a kidney and bladder ultrasound for obstruction; a frequency-volume chart once voiding. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); AUA White Paper on Nonneurogenic Chronic Urinary Retention: Consensus Definition, Treatment Algorithm, and Outcome End Points (2017); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>PSA only after shared decision-making: it helps estimate prostate size and choose treatment; screening for prostate cancer is not recommended at his age, and he would not want treatment for a slow-growing cancer; drawn before the catheter is placed, because retention and catheters raise it. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); Prostate Cancer: Screening (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Indwelling urethral catheter to drain the bladder, with a closed drainage system, the bag below the bladder, daily washing with soap and water, no routine antibiotics or bladder washouts, no treatment of bacteria in the urine without symptoms, a change every four weeks, and same-day review for fever, pain, blood, or a blocked catheter. Guideline: AUA White Paper on Nonneurogenic Chronic Urinary Retention: Consensus Definition, Treatment Algorithm, and Outcome End Points (2017); Diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of catheter-associated urinary tract infection in adults: 2009 International Clinical Practice Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2009) [S]</item><item>Tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime for at least three days and usually several weeks before a trial without catheter; fall precautions: take it after the evening meal, rise slowly, sit to pass urine at night, and use a night light. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023) [S]</item><item>Urology consultation for chronic urinary retention with a raised creatinine: confirm the cause, judge the risk, plan the trial without catheter, advise on a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, and set out the procedures that suit a frail 79-year-old who wants to avoid an operation. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); AUA White Paper on Nonneurogenic Chronic Urinary Retention: Consensus Definition, Treatment Algorithm, and Outcome End Points (2017); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Medication review with the AGS Beers Criteria: avoid anticholinergic drugs for bladder symptoms while in retention, first-generation antihistamines, and benzodiazepines; avoid nonselective alpha-blockers; review his herbal preparation with the pharmacist for interactions and unlabeled ingredients. Guideline: American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); Lead, mercury, and arsenic in US- and Indian-manufactured Ayurvedic medicines sold via the Internet (2008) [S]</item><item>Monitor on the CMID and AUA schedule: IPSS at every visit once voiding; post-void residual with each trial without catheter and after it; creatinine with retention; PSA at baseline and six months after starting finasteride, doubled for interpretation; response to tamsulosin at four to twelve weeks and to finasteride at three to six months. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026) [S]</item><item>Geriatric assessment before any surgical decision: frailty, gait and falls, orthostatic blood pressure on tamsulosin, cognition, mood, delirium risk, nutrition, and what matters most to him. Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023) [S]</item></list></text>

